### PostgreSQL Compatibility: PL/pgSQL Static Compilation Error on PG 14 and Older

In PostgreSQL, PL/pgSQL compiles the entire `DO` block before execution. If this script is run on PostgreSQL 14 or older, the compiler will fail immediately with a syntax/option error when parsing `WITH (security_invoker = true)` (which was introduced in PG 15), even though the branch is guarded by `if is_pg15`.

To prevent compile-time failures on older PostgreSQL versions, any version-specific SQL syntax must be executed dynamically using `EXECUTE`.

Additionally, using dynamic SQL allows you to conditionally append the `WITH (security_invoker = true)` clause as a string, which completely eliminates over 100 lines of duplicated view definitions between the `IF` and `ELSE` branches!

Example:
```sql
do $projection_views$
declare
  is_pg15 boolean := current_setting('server_version_num')::int >= 150000;
  with_options text := '';
begin
  if is_pg15 then
    with_options := ' with (security_invoker = true)';
  end if;

  execute 'create view private.kg_v1_b0_query_projection_question_runtime_availability' || with_options || ' as ...';
end
$projection_views$;
```

